{
  "image_name": "94e29a32173ebb39f353c6597dc4d061.jpg",
  "prompt_text": "The figure summarizes several designs for fibre-shaped energy devices built around flexible core–shell architectures. Overall, it shows how a central fibre, often a rubber or metal wire, is coated with functional layers such as MWCNTs and gel electrolytes to create bendable supercapacitors, lithium-ion batteries, and integrated energy-harvesting/storage fibres.\n\nOne concept uses an elastic rubber fibre coated with carbon nanotubes and gel electrolyte, forming a segmented, stretchable cable-like supercapacitor that can bend and elongate. Another design compares twisted fibre electrodes for different chemistries: a supercapacitor based on MWCNT/OMC and lithium-ion battery fibres using MWCNT/LTO and MWCNT/LMO. The lower schematics emphasize the different charge-storage mechanisms, with electron flow through the fibre and Li+ moving between battery electrodes through the electrolyte.\n\nA further example combines photoelectric conversion with storage: dye-adsorbed TiO2 nanotubes on Ti wire act as a DSSC-like photoanode, paired with a storage fibre containing TiO2 nanotubes and an MWCNT fibre. The final schematic integrates these functions into one coaxial fibre device, showing separated regions for the supercapacitor and DSSC, electrolytes, electrodes, and an external resistor, highlighting a multifunctional wearable power fibre.",
